Subtask 3.3: Simulate Node Failure - Method 1 (Node Drain)
Simulate a planned node failure using node drain:

# Identify a node with running VMs
TARGET_NODE=$(oc get vmi failover-test-vm-1 -o jsonpath='{.status.nodeName}')
echo "Target node for drain: $TARGET_NODE"

# Start monitoring in another terminal (optional)
# ./monitor-vms.sh

# Drain the node (this simulates planned maintenance)
oc adm drain $TARGET_NODE --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --force

# Watch VM migration
oc get vmi -l test-group=failover -o wide -w
Subtask 3.4: Verify VM Migration
Check that VMs have been successfully migrated:

# Check VM status after drain
oc get vmi -l test-group=failover -o wide

# Check events for migration details
oc get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp' | grep -i migration

# Verify VMs are still running
oc get vms -l test-group=failover
Subtask 3.5: Restore Node and Test Recovery
Restore the drained node and verify normal operations:

# Uncordon the node
oc adm uncordon $TARGET_NODE

# Verify node is ready
oc get nodes

# Check if new VMs can be scheduled on the restored node
oc apply -f - << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: recovery-test-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: recovery-test-vm
    spec:
      nodeSelector:
        kubernetes.io/hostname: TARGET_NODE_NAME
      domain:
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
        machine:
          type: ""
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 256Mi
            cpu: 250m
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
EOF
Subtask 3.6: Simulate Node Failure - Method 2 (Node Shutdown)
For a more realistic failure simulation, we can simulate an unplanned node shutdown:

# Mark a node as unschedulable
TARGET_NODE=$(oc get vmi failover-test-vm-2 -o jsonpath='{.status.nodeName}')
oc adm cordon $TARGET_NODE

# Simulate node failure by marking it as NotReady
# Note: In a real scenario, this would happen automatically
oc patch node $TARGET_NODE -p '{"status":{"conditions":[{"type":"Ready","status":"False","reason":"NodeShutdown","message":"Node is shutting down"}]}}'

# Watch for automatic VM eviction
oc get vmi -l test-group=failover -o wide -w
Subtask 3.7: Configure VM Eviction Policies
Create a VM with specific eviction policies for better HA:

# Create file: ha-vm-eviction-policy.yaml
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: ha-vm-eviction-policy
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: ha-vm-eviction-policy
      annotations:
        kubevirt.io/evictionStrategy: LiveMigrate
    spec:
      evictionStrategy: LiveMigrate
      domain:
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
        machine:
          type: ""
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
            cpu: 1
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
Apply the eviction policy configuration:

# Create VM with eviction policy
oc apply -f ha-vm-eviction-policy.yaml

# Verify the eviction strategy
oc describe vmi ha-vm-eviction-policy | grep -i eviction
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: VMs Not Scheduling Due to Taints
Problem: VMs remain in Pending state due to node taints.

Solution:

# Check node taints
oc describe nodes | grep -i taint

# Add appropriate tolerations to VM spec
# Or remove unnecessary taints
oc adm taint nodes worker-1 maintenance=true:NoSchedule-
Issue 2: Anti-Affinity Rules Too Restrictive
Problem: VMs cannot be scheduled due to strict anti-affinity rules.

Solution:

# Use preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution instead of required
# Or adjust the topologyKey to be less restrictive
Issue 3: Migration Failures
Problem: VMs fail to migrate during node drain.

Solution:

# Check migration status
oc get virtualmachineinstancemigration

# Check events for error details
oc get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

# Ensure sufficient resources on target nodes
oc describe nodes
Issue 4: Slow Failover Response
Problem: VMs take too long to failover after node failure.

Solution:

# Adjust node monitor grace period (cluster-level setting)
# Configure faster health checks
# Use LiveMigrate eviction strategy for faster response
